\" Generated by script2man from auto-useradd
.TH auto-useradd 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-useradd - Platform-independent useradd tool

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-useradd [--no-local-pw] user_name user_id group_name gid gecos shell
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
user_name   Username for the new user
user_id     Numeric user ID
group_name  Primary group name
gid         Primary numeric group ID for the new user
gecos       Text description (usually full name, maybe title)
shell       Login shell (e.g. /bin/sh)
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-useradd
command provides a platform-independend,
non-interactive interface for adding
users on a variety of different operating systems.

Native tools for this vary widely across operating systems, creating
hurdles for those who want to automate management of
multiple different systems.
Auto-admin(1) thus serves and
a compatibility layer, removing platform-specific code from
numerous higher-level projects.

If the optional --no-local-pw flag is given, a local password will
not be set.  This is useful where ldap(3) or AD (active directory)
are used for authenticating over the network.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-useradd joeuser 2000 joeuser 2000 "Joe User, regular guy" /bin/tcsh
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/passwd
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-adduser(8), passwd(1)

